 Applications (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.08019v1)\nciteturn31579view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.08019v1\",\"lineno\":25}); Total lines: 166\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 8 Feb 2026]\nL9: # Title:The Rise of Sparse Mixture-of-Experts: A Survey from Algorithmic Foundations to Decentralized Architectures and Vertical Domain Applications\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Dong Pan , cite6†Bingtao Li , cite7†Yongsheng Zheng , cite8†Jiren Ma , cite9†Victor Fei L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled The Rise of Sparse Mixture-of-Experts: A Survey from Algorithmic Foundations to Decentralized Architectures and Vertical Domain Applications, by Dong Pan and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:The sparse Mixture of Experts(MoE) architecture has evolved as a powerful approach for scaling deep learning models to more parameters with comparable computation cost. As an important branch of large language model(LLM), MoE model only activate a subset of experts based on a routing network. This sparse conditional computation mechanism significantly improves computational efficiency, paving a promising path for greater scalability and cost-efficiency.\nL17: It not only enhance downstream applications such as natural language processing, computer vision, and multimodal in various horizontal domains, but also exhibit broad applicability across vertical domains. Despite the growing popularity and application of MoE models across various domains, there lacks a systematic exploration of recent advancements of MoE in many important fields. Existing surveys on MoE suffer from limitations such as lack coverage or none extensively exploration of key areas.\nL18: This survey seeks to fill these gaps. In this paper, Firstly, we examine the foundational principles of MoE, with an in-depth exploration of its core components-the routing network and expert network. Subsequently, we extend beyond the centralized paradigm to the decentralized paradigm, which unlocks the immense untapped potential of decentralized infrastructure, enables democratization of MoE development for broader communities, and delivers greater scalability and cost-efficiency.\nL19: Furthermore we focus on exploring its vertical domain applications. Finally, we also identify key challenges and promising future research directions. To the best of our knowledge, this survey is currently the most comprehensive review in the field of MoE. We aim for this article to serve as a valuable resource for both researchers and practitioners, enabling them to navigate and stay up-to-date with the latest advancements.\nL20: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2602.08019 [cs.LG]\nL22:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2602.08019v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL23:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.08019†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: Journal reference:  | Journal of Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence 5 (2025) 25-41\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Bingtao Li [cite15†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Sun, 8 Feb 2026 15:39:10 UTC (831 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled The Rise of Sparse Mixture-of-Experts: A Survey from Algorithmic Foundations to Decentralized Architectures and Vertical Domain Applications, by Dong Pan and 4 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite10†View PDF L37:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite16†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite17†view license L41: ### Current br